<Layout title="What Should Be Included in a Construction Document Set? | ATECT Architecture" description="A practical overview of the information commonly included in architectural construction documents for residential and commercial projects.">
  <article class="article"><header><p class="eyebrow">Construction</p><h1>What Should Be Included in a Construction Document Set?</h1><p class="lede">A construction document set should communicate the design clearly enough for the project team to understand scope, dimensions, materials, and construction intent.</p></header>
  <section><h2>Common components</h2><ul><li>Cover or general information sheets</li><li>Floor plans with dimensions and key notes</li><li>Exterior elevations</li><li>Building and wall sections where required</li><li>Details for important assemblies and conditions</li><li>Door, window, finish, or equipment schedules when applicable</li><li>Reflected ceiling or interior documentation when applicable</li><li>Material and construction notes coordinated with the drawings</li></ul></section>
  <section><h2>The set should match the project</h2><p>Not every project requires the same drawings. A small renovation, a major addition, and a new commercial building can have very different documentation needs. The appropriate set depends on scope, complexity, permitting requirements, construction method, and the information needed by the project team.</p></section>
  <section><h2>From design to construction</h2><p>The goal is not simply to produce more sheets. The goal is coordinated information that helps owners, contractors, consultants, and permitting authorities understand the project.</p></section>
  <section class="callout"><h2>Building or renovating?</h2><p>ATECT Architecture develops residential and commercial construction documentation with the project scope and construction process in mind.</p><a class="button dark" href="/contact">Discuss Your Project</a></section>
  </article>
</Layout>
